In Ladybird, the default selection keeps the selected text’s own color
unless its contrast with the selection, composited over the backdrop, is
below 4.5:1; then the text is drawn in black or white. No other engine
checks the text’s own color this way. Chromium on macOS keeps the text’s
color, since LayoutThemeMac::SupportsSelectionForegroundColors() returns
false, but on Linux always draws it in the theme’s selection foreground
color, through HighlightStyleUtils::DefaultForegroundColor(). WebKit on
macOS keeps the text’s color in a light appearance and uses AppKit’s
selectedTextColor in a dark one; WebKitGTK keeps it. And Gecko’s
nsTextPaintStyle::EnsureSufficientContrast() swaps the selection’s
foreground and background colors where the background is too close to
the page’s, by a measure of brightness difference, not contrast ratio.
